                           The reference path

        The CPU path implements every restraint in the table.  It is the
reference against which the others are read, and where a restraint's
documented behaviour is defined.


File: Restraints, Node: OpenMM, Up: Top, Previous: CPU, Next: BLaDE

                 What the OpenMM interface translates

        setup_restraints in source/openmm/omm_restraint.F90 wires five
restraint types and no others:

  setup_harm_restraints        CONS HARM
  setup_pcharm_restraints      principal component harmonic
  setup_resd_restraints        CONS RESD
  setup_cons_dihe              CONS DIHE
  setup_mmfpgeo_restraints     MMFP GEO

Limits within those:

  - CONS HARM: only the absolute form with XSCALE = YSCALE = ZSCALE = 1 and
    a positive exponent.  A restraint set with scaling or a non-positive
    exponent is not applied.
  - MMFP GEO: sphere, cylinder and plane, in the inside, outside and
    symmetric forms, plus the centre-of-mass distance form.

Everything else in the table -- NOE, CONS HMCM, DMC, RXNCOR, ADUMB, BLOCK
CATS, SHAP, RGY, PULL and the rest -- is not translated.  An active
restraint of those types does not reach the OpenMM System, so it does not
act during dynamics, even though an ENERgy call still reports its energy.
That is the NO-DYN column entry; see *note Table:: for the measurement.

One measured defect inside the supported set: a CONS RESD restraint that
works under the CPU code and DOMDEC kills the run under OpenMM when the
system is periodic with PME.  The process dies with no CHARMM message at
all (exit 133).  The same RESD form in the non-periodic testcase
c39test/omm_resdtest.inp passes, so the trigger is the periodic system
rather than the restraint form.  Not yet root-caused.


File: Restraints, Node: BLaDE, Up: Top, Previous: OpenMM, Next: Domdec

                  What the BLaDE interface translates

        source/blade_api/blade.F90 adds restraints through blade_add_noe,
blade_add_harmonic, blade_add_boRest, blade_add_anRest, blade_add_diRest
and blade_add_resd.

Limits within those:

  - CONS HARM: as for OpenMM, only the absolute form with unit scaling and
    a positive exponent.
  - NOE: NOESCA must be 1; i and j groups must hold exactly one atom each;
    time averaging (NOETCN) is refused.
  - MMFP GEO: only the centre-of-mass bond, angle and dihedral forms
    (IGEO -1, -4, -5), and only the harmonic form (JGEO 3).  Groups must
    hold one atom.  The geometric shapes are refused outright.
  - CONS RESD: only a linear combination of exactly two distances.

BLaDE computes the BLOCK CATS restraint in its own energy term rather than
through the CHARMM one, which is why the table says "own" rather than "yes".


File: Restraints, Node: Domdec, Up: Top, Previous: BLaDE, Next: Reporting

                       DOMDEC and DOMDEC_GPU

        DOMDEC is not a translation layer.  Restraints are computed by the
same CPU code; what DOMDEC changes is which node computes them and whether
the result is correct there.  Three groups:

Refused outright.  These stop the run with WRNDIE:

  Shape restraints, puckering restraints, internal coordinate restraints
  (source/energy/energy.F90), and the relative, best-fit and RMSD forms of
  CONS HARM (source/energy/ecnstr.F90).

Handled by the constraint machinery.  Under a direct/reciprocal split,
q_cons_node is set only on a reciprocal node, so energy_recip in
source/energy/energy_util.F90 is the only path that computes these.  The
values travel back to the direct nodes packed into auxdata by
auxdata_kernel and unpacked by read_from_auxdata.  Those two routines are
hand-mirrored: a term the sender packs and the receiver does not expect
shifts every value after it.  The terms with a slot are

  adaptive umbrella, CONS DIHE, NOE, CONS RESD, RXNCOR umbrella,
  the distance matrix, CONS HMCM, and the density bias

BLOCK CATS has no slot of its own and borrows the NOE one, which is why it
is marked "part"; that borrow is deliberate and tracked for replacement.

Note that the slot holding eterm(charm) carries the density bias, not the
CONS HARM restraint.  CONS HARM needs no slot: ECNSTR is DOMDEC-aware and
sums over the local atom list on each node.

Everything else.  Evaluated in the ordinary energy path with no DOMDEC
guard at all.  Some carry a rank-zero guard (MMFP GEO, radius of gyration)
and some carry nothing.  They are marked "part" because they are outside
both mechanisms above: nothing refuses them and nothing arranges for them
under a split.  What each one actually reports in parallel has not been
measured.

Without a direct/reciprocal split the constraint-crossing question does not
arise, but the refusals still apply.

