         What happens when a restraint is not supported

        There is no single behaviour.  Three different things happen
depending on which restraint and which engine, and only one of them stops
the run:

  1. WRNDIE.  BLaDE refuses its unsupported NOE, MMFP and RESD cases this
     way, at a level that terminates.

  2. A note in the output.  Both OpenMM and BLaDE report an unsupported
     CONS HARM variant with a plain write at PRNLEV greater than 2.  Since
     PRNLEV defaults to 5 this does appear in normal output, but it is not
     a warning: it has no warning level, it does not raise the warning
     count, and nothing downstream treats it as a problem.  In a long run
     it scrolls past.

  3. Nothing.  A restraint type the engine does not mention anywhere -- NOE
     under OpenMM, CONS HMCM under either, and most of the lower half of
     the table -- produces no message of any kind.  The run proceeds
     without the restraint and nothing in the output says so.

Case 3 is the one that produces wrong results with no evidence, and it
covers the majority of the table.


File: Restraints, Node: Doc drift, Up: Top, Previous: Reporting, Next: Verifying

          Where the other doc files disagree with the code

        Recorded as found, so that the disagreements are fixed rather than
rediscovered:

  - doc/openmm.info describes three restraint types (a CONS HARM subset,
    CONS RESD and CONS DIHE).  The code supports five.  MMFP GEO and the
    principal component harmonic restraints are not mentioned anywhere in
    that file, so the entire MMFP GEO path through OpenMM is undocumented.

  - doc/blade.info says "a few restraints are implemented, including
    absolute position restraints, and NOE distance restraints", and that
    only restraints involving a single atom or pair of atoms are supported.
    The code also handles CONS DIHE, CONS RESD and the MMFP centre-of-mass
    bond, angle and dihedral restraints.

  - doc/domdec.info's list of supported constraints matches the code.  It
    does not mention that BLOCK CATS borrows the NOE slot.


